From fcb5b47738a8605776e876b946b7cebfe738ec66 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Thomas <chris.thomas@antimatter-studios.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 15:19:24 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(desktop): Touch ID unlock, and the bundle identifier the
 app should have had
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Unlocking asks for a fingerprint instead of a typed password, when the vault
has one stored for it. `LAContext.evaluatePolicy` shows the system prompt and
the password comes out of the login keychain — the same entry `trove keychain
save` writes, so enrolling in either place serves both.

Deliberately honest about what this is: the fingerprint is a gate in this
application's code, not a cryptographic binding. Anything that can already read
your login keychain can reach the password without a prompt. The strong version
puts the password in the data-protection keychain behind
`kSecAttrAccessControl`, where the Secure Enclave itself refuses to release it —
and that needs a team-prefixed `keychain-access-groups` entitlement, which needs
a provisioning profile. Measured, not assumed: an .app signed Developer ID with
that entitlement and no profile is SIGKILLed at exec, exactly as a bare binary
is. Only the storage layer changes when the profile exists; the prompt and
everything above it stay.

The button appears only when the Mac can do biometrics AND this vault is
enrolled, both asked when the screen draws — biometry goes away with the lid
shut on a clamshell setup, and the entry can be removed from Keychain Access at
any time. Enrolment happens after a password the vault has just accepted, never
from what someone typed into a box, and a cancelled prompt returns to the
password field rather than showing an error, because cancelling is a decision.

The bundle identifier becomes `com.antimatterstudios.trove`, matching the other
applications in this account. macOS keys an app's config directory by that
identifier, so the rename would have orphaned the registered-vault list and the
agent settings — the app would have come up looking factory-new with the user's
vaults apparently gone. `migrate_legacy_config` copies both files across on
first run, copying rather than moving so an older build still works and nothing
is destroyed if it goes wrong.

Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WUWwBLxZG6iVEcmzDrTCsQ
